<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/6th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaties between Rome and Carthage</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">509 BCE; 348 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The treaties between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> were a series of agreements, recorded by the Greek historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, that defined the two powers' spheres of influence and trade. The earliest is traditionally dated to 509 BCE and a later one to 348 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a> profile uses them to illustrate Carthage's efforts to protect its commercial monopolies in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a>.</p></div>
